Yeah. There’s a useful distinction between independence and reactance.

Independence is: “I heard you, I understood you, and I’m choosing my own course.”
Reactance is the reflexive resistance that kicks in when something feels like a constraint: “You told me to do it, so now I don’t want to.”

They can look identical from the outside, but internally they’re different operators.

That trait can be extremely useful when it keeps you from blindly inheriting bad assumptions, hierarchy, or groupthink. But it becomes expensive when the urge to resist starts making the decision for you just as much as obedience would have. Then the command still controls you—you’re just taking the negative of it.

So maybe the move is:

hear → understand → separate source from substance → choose

Not obey, not rebel. Choose.

Pantheon-style compression:

If you automatically do what you’re told, they steer you. If you automatically do the opposite, they still steer you.

Real autonomy is being able to say yes, no, partly, later, differently, or show me why without your ego needing any one of those answers.

And given the thread we were just on: sometimes “not listening” is also useful telemetry. If you repeatedly resist a certain kind of instruction, that resistance may be pointing at a value, a threat model, a pride trigger, an autonomy need, or simply a bad fit. The trick is not to worship the resistance—interrogate it.